# A WPF list box opens with focus on the LIST and no item focused, so the first
# arrow press moves onto the first item without selecting it. Announcing that
# item exactly as if it were selected gives the user no way to tell "here is
# where you are" from "here is what is chosen" — and it sounded like the list
# had skipped a step, because the same item was read twice.
#
# Gated on Selectable, which is only set when the element actually exposes the
# selection-item pattern. Without that gate, a provider that never reports
# selection would have every one of its items announced as "not selected".

node here     ListItem "General" pos=1 size=7 states=Selectable
node chosen   ListItem "Speech"  pos=2 size=7 states=Selectable|Selected
node opaque   ListItem "Music"   pos=3 size=7

focus here
drain
focus chosen
drain
focus opaque

expect
  General, list item, not selected, 1 of 7
  Speech, list item, 2 of 7
  Music, list item, 3 of 7
